  <section class="chapter wrap" id="self-check">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Self-check</p>
      <h2>Self-check: malware, scams, and staying safe</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>Use this to see what you remember from the whole lesson, then go back to any part you missed.</p>

      <h3>Do not confuse: summary</h3>
      <p>These pairs are often mixed up. The right column says what makes the two different:</p>
      <div class="scroll-x"><table class="tbl">
        <thead><tr><th>These two...</th><th>...are different because</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><b>Virus vs. worm</b></td><td>needs a person to open an infected file vs. spreads by itself over a network</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Trojan horse vs. virus</b></td><td>pretends to be useful and does not copy itself vs. copies itself into other files</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Bot vs. botnet</b></td><td>one infected device vs. many bots controlled by one botherder</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>DoS vs. DDoS</b></td><td>a flood from one source vs. a flood from many devices at once</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Data breach vs. identity theft</b></td><td>data is seen or stolen vs. stolen data is used to pretend to be someone</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Phishing vs. spear phishing</b></td><td>the same bait for many people vs. a message made for one person or group</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Smishing vs. vishing vs. quishing</b></td><td>by text message vs. by voice call vs. by QR code</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Phishing vs. pharming</b></td><td>you are tricked into tapping a fake link vs. you are redirected even when you type the right address</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Digital certificate vs. digital signature</b></td><td>proves a website owns its address vs. proves who signed a file and that it was not changed</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Cyberbullying vs. cyberstalking</b></td><td>repeatedly hurting or shaming someone vs. repeatedly following or threatening someone so they feel afraid</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Doxxing vs. identity theft</b></td><td>exposing someone's private information vs. using it to pretend to be them</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>

      <h3>What would you do?</h3>
      <p>Choose the safest action.</p>
      <Quiz id="quiz-scen" questions={scenarios} />

      <h3>Try it at home</h3>
      <p>No need to submit anything:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Check that automatic updates are on for your phone and laptop, and install any waiting updates.</li>
        <li>Back up your important school files using the 3-2-1 rule. At least one copy should be in the cloud or on a drive you keep unplugged.</li>
        <li>Open your social media privacy settings. Who can see your posts, your friends list, and your tagged photos? Remove any posts that show your ID or home.</li>
        <li>Agree on a family code word for emergency calls, and save hotline <b>1326</b> in your phone and your family's phones.</li>
      </ol>

      <h3>Final quiz</h3>
      <p>Twelve questions: eleven from this lesson and one that mixes both lessons.</p>
      <Quiz id="quiz-final" questions={final} />

      <div class="remember">
        <p class="note-label">Key takeaways</p>
        <ol>
          <li><b class="term">Computer sabotage</b> damages or disables systems. Its four forms are <b>botnets</b>, <b>malware</b> (virus, worm, Trojan horse, ransomware, spyware, mobile malware), <b>denial of service</b> attacks, and <b>data or website alteration</b>. Protect yourself with updates, antivirus, official app stores, care with files and USB drives, firewalls, and 3-2-1 backups. Organizations add staff training, MFA, tested backups, and an incident response plan.</li>
          <li><b>Online theft</b> includes data breaches and <b class="term">identity theft</b>. Most of it starts with <b class="term">social engineering</b>, such as phishing, spear phishing, smishing, vishing, quishing, and scams such as shopping, task, investment, love, money mule, and deepfake scams. Scammers often add two tricks, called <b class="term">pharming</b> (redirecting you to a fake website) and <b class="term">spoofing</b> (faking the sender).</li>
          <li>The main <b>red flags</b> are a rush or threat, requests for passwords, PINs, or OTPs, strange links or senders, offers too good to be true, and money to a different name. Go to the source yourself, use MFA, and check certificates and addresses.</li>
          <li>The main <b>personal safety</b> risks are cyberbullying, cyberstalking, sextortion, doxxing, and grooming. Keep accounts private, share less, block, report, save evidence, and tell someone you trust.</li>
          <li>The main <b>laws</b> are RA 8792, RA 9995, RA 10175, RA 10173, RA 10627, RA 11313, RA 11930, RA 11934, and RA 12010 (AFASA). After a scam, call your bank or e-wallet, then hotline <b>1326</b>.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <p><b>Next:</b> Information systems and system development is coming soon. Missed something from Lesson 1? <a href={L1}>Go back to Lesson 1: keeping intruders out</a>. <a href={base + course.href}>Back to the IT 110 lessons</a>.</p>
    </div>
